<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">United Tribes of New Zealand</h1><p class="st-meta">1835 CE – 1840 CE · capital Waitangi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A confederation of northern Māori chiefs who declared New Zealand's independence in 1835, five years before the Treaty of Waitangi.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The British Resident, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-busby">James Busby</a>, encouraged northern chiefs to choose a national flag in 1834 so that <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>-built ships could be registered. In 1835, alarmed by the French adventurer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-thierry">Charles de Thierry's</a> claim to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, Busby gathered 34 chiefs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waitangi-northland">Waitangi</a> to sign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/declaration-of-the-independence-of-new-zealand">He Whakaputanga</a>, the Declaration of Independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, on 28 October.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The declaration vested sovereign power in the hereditary chiefs collectively and proposed an annual congress at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waitangi-northland">Waitangi</a> to make laws, with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> asked to act as protector. The congress never met, and the confederation existed mainly on paper and in its flag. By 1839 the number of signatories had grown to 52, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waikato">Waikato</a> chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potatau-te-wherowhero">Te Wherowhero</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>, which had acknowledged the declaration, negotiated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-waitangi">Treaty of Waitangi</a> with the chiefs in 1840 and established the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-new-zealand">Colony of New Zealand</a>. Whether the treaty transferred the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> the declaration asserted remains central to debate in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/united-tribes-of-new-zealand.jpg" alt="United Tribes of New Zealand" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. Greentubing assumed (based on copyright claims). · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_United_Tribes_of_New_Zealand.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1835 CE – 1840 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Waitangi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Māori religion, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Māori</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>New Zealand</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Confederation of the United Tribes of New Zealand, Te Wakaminenga</dd></dl></aside></div>
